A||N||5||Seanh||Front Spring Compression?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||06-29-2001||01:28 AM||||Ok, I'm ready to re-install the stock front drivers side spring. I'v struggled with it all night, and it ain't going in with a damn crowbar and my foot! Is there a spring compressor that will fit in there? The only stores here are Advance Auto, NAPA, and Wally world. Do any of them sell/rent somthing that would work? How the hell do you do this?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||06-29-2001||02:08 AM||||You need to push the lower are almost straight down then we used a long pipe (like the handle of a floor jack) to set the spring into the seat on the lower arm Then using the floor jack to raise the control arm up. We also used a chain around the spring and frame as a safety in case the jack slipped out. You don't want to be in the path of it if it slips out!||||reg||1
Z||000002||no_rice||06-29-2001||02:19 AM||||exactly,sean. and how many "explosives" do you have on stock?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||06-29-2001||10:03 AM||||Compress the spring on a bench, then wire it up with several loops of heavy mechanic's wire in each of three or four places to hold it when you remove the spring compressor. Do not loop the wire around the end coils or you won't be able to get it out when you reinstall the spring.<P>Once the spring is compressed and wired up, stick it on the upper spring perch, reassemble the suspension, and cut the wires.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||06-29-2001||10:38 AM||||Thats a pretty good idea there. Since I have no way to compress the springs, I am going to try Oreif's idea first, and if I can't get that to work I will go down to NAPA and see what they have for spring compressors, and try bench compressing it. I read in a previous post that someone used a tool which was designed to lower your springs by keeping them compressed, and he compessed the springs on the bench, installed that lock on the springs, and reinstalled. Then once the spring was compressed in the suspension, remove the locks. I would use that idea if I could find some of those locally, but I doubt there would be any here. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||DRH||06-29-2001||01:11 PM||||I don't think you'll have enough room for any conventional spring compressor. The 88 shop manual says to attach the ball joint side and use a floor jack to raise the arm up until you can put the frame to control arm bolts in.<P>In my first couple of attempts the control arm kept wanting to go toward the outside of the car and slip off the jack. I hooked a cable puller from the control arm to the frame on the far side and used that to guide the arm straight up. Worked great then!!! Doesn't take a lot of pressure a friend with a rope would probably work too. Also, I used a big 3 ton floor jack. A smaller jack may no be stable enough to be safe.<P><BR>Whatever you do... BE CAREFUL. When compressed enough to bolt everything up the spring has the potental to do some serious injury if it gets loose!!!<P>||||reg||1
